Step 1: Identify the blocking application handle

db2pd -db <DBNAME> -locks showlocks wait

Look for the AppHandl (application handle) of the blocking session. The db2pd -locks output shows lock holders and waiters
Example:
AppHandl Status
12345   Lock Waiter 
67890   Lock Holder

Here, 67890 is the blocking application handle.

Step 2: Map the application handle to a PID
db2pd -db <DBNAME> -agents app=67890

This displays agent information, including the associated OS PID
Typical output:

AppHandl AgentID TID    PID
67890    1024    56789  23456
PID = 23456


Step 3: Verify on the operating system
ps -fp 23456

This helps identify the DB2 EDU or client process involved.

Step 4: Force the blocking session (if appropriate)
db2 force application (67890)



db2 list applications |grep -E toad | wc -l
